For printed circuit boards, attention must be paid to the soldering requirements for electronic components; before assembly, the overall circuit of the machine must be basically finalized, and the assembly method of the components on the printed circuit board must be determined according to the volume and the size of the enclosure. Layout generally starts with the larger components such as the power transformer and integrated circuits; connectors are also larger components and are usually laid out first, while small components are laid out flexibly according to the gaps. The assembly methods of diodes, inductors and resistance-capacitance components generally include the upright type, the lying type and the mixed type; upright mounting means that resistors, capacitors and diodes are mounted vertically on the printed circuit board, and its advantage is that more components are accommodated per unit area and the arrangement is compact. The soldering sequence should follow the principle of small before large and low before high, otherwise, after the large or high devices are soldered, the soldering quality of the small devices will be affected; at the same time, unnecessary component pins need to be cut off, and the surface of the printed circuit board must be cleaned. The circuit board repair procedure applies to printed circuit boards, and during maintenance the fault can be located by troubleshooting level by level according to this procedure. If the soldering essentials are not mastered, virtual soldering is easily produced;

When using an electric soldering iron, pay attention to the high temperature of the metal part to prevent personal burns; the power cord should be checked frequently for exposed copper wire, and if there is any, it should be wrapped with insulating tape; when the electric soldering iron is not in use, the power should be cut off in time and it should be allowed to cool naturally. These contents constitute the precautions for using an electric soldering iron; marine electrical equipment requires the precautions for using an electric soldering iron in soldering and maintenance operations to ensure operational safety. The multimeter is widely used in maintenance, and a multimeter can be used to measure resistance, voltage, capacitance, diodes and transistors. Voltage is one of the instrument readings that need to be checked in marine electrical fault diagnosis; voltage can be measured with a multimeter or a voltmeter; the generator failing to build up voltage is an optional top event when constructing a fault tree; overvoltage is one of the causes of motor overheating in AC electric drive systems. The timer is a digital logic circuit application component used in household appliances, and in marine living equipment it is often used to control the start and stop of electric fans, the washing and spinning programs of washing machines, and the defrosting cycle of cold storage; the electric fan contains a timer, and in the troubleshooting of electric fan faults, if the fan sometimes rotates and sometimes does not, one of the possible causes is timer failure, and the remedy is to replace the timer; in the troubleshooting of a washing machine that does not work after the power is switched on, it is necessary to check whether the timer wiring is loose or disconnected, whether the timer knob slips, and whether the contacts are in good contact.

Electronic equipment uses a large number of various passive and active components, and judging their performance and handling faults is common content in maintenance work. The variable resistor is one of the three major classifications of resistors. If the pointer cannot return to the infinity position, the leakage resistance is the reading multiplied by the range. The circuit symbol is VD, with designations such as VD5; marine electrical equipment uses transistors, and transistors will suffer transistor faults. The greater the pointer deflection, the more unstable the performance. Marine electrical equipment uses integrated circuits. Marine electrical equipment uses power supply circuits. The power supply section is a component of the PLC, responsible for converting AC and DC power into the three types of DC working power required by the internal circuits; the first type of power supply supplies the integrated circuit and is +5V and +15V DC power. If the soldering essentials are not mastered, virtual soldering is easily produced; both the analog multimeter and the digital multimeter belong to multimeters. The multimeter uses resistance, voltage, current, capacitance, diodes, transistors, thyristors, photosensitive devices and thermocouples for measurement, and the clamp-on ammeter uses current. The measurable performance of voltage is the voltage value, and the measuring tools are the multimeter and the voltmeter; the measurable performance of current is the current value, and the measuring tools are the multimeter, the ammeter and the clamp-on ammeter.

When the brush is found to wear quickly and the commutator surface is rough or has burn marks, the commutator surface should be ground; carbon powder produced by brush wear accumulating in the commutator grooves may cause short circuit between segments, and must be cleaned regularly. For brushed motors, excessive brush wear is one of the common faults, on a par with stator winding open circuit and short circuit; when the electric drill fails to start due to poor contact caused by brush wear, the brush should be replaced directly. After replacing the brushes of a DC motor, if the contact area is less than 70%, the contact surface between the brush and the commutator must be ground; when grinding newly replaced brushes, the brush must be placed in the brush holder, and 00# abrasive cloth wrapped around the commutator surface and pulled back and forth. The brush holder is the device that holds the brush; when grinding the contact surface between the brush and the commutator, the brush must be placed in the brush holder for the operation, and when checking the contact surface of a motor in operation, the brush may be taken out of the brush holder for observation. For marine motors under long-term 15° list, 7.5° trim and 22.5° roll, the brush holder connecting parts must be ensured not to loosen; too tight a fit clearance between the brush and the brush holder will cause jamming, causing excessive brush sparks; a loose brush holder causes the brushes to be out of line, affecting commutation and increasing sparks. In DC motor maintenance, it must be ensured that the brush and commutator are in good contact and that the surface is clean and smooth. Before dismantling the end cover of a DC motor, the bearing outer cover must first be removed; bearing installation commonly uses the driving-in method or the shrink-fitting method, and the shrink-fitting method requires heating the bearing in oil at about 100℃ and then fitting it onto the shaft journal. Checking bearing fault requires the use of the bearing, and checking the lubrication condition likewise requires the use of the bearing;

Assembly precautions require that the assembly steps be the reverse of disassembly, that is, what was removed last is installed first, and what was removed first is installed last. Screws must not be tightened with excessive force to prevent twisting off, and screws with different pitches or sizes are strictly prohibited from being forced in; the spring washers on the screws must be retained and must not be discarded. During assembly, the insulation of the winding ends must be protected, and after completion, turn the rotor forward and reverse to check its flexibility and abnormal noise; tightening the end cover and bearing cover screws should be carried out in sequence in the diagonal direction to prevent damage to the spigot and eccentricity; at the start of assembly, high-pressure air should be used to blow out the motor interior to ensure no foreign matter or forgotten parts remain inside. Protecting the winding during assembly requires attention to protecting the winding ends during assembly and not damaging the insulation; after assembly is completed, the rotor should be turned several revolutions forward and reverse to check flexibility and abnormal noise; at the start of assembly, high-pressure air is generally used to blow out the motor interior to remove foreign matter; all removed parts must be reinstalled, and if any are missing or extra, the cause must be found and the work redone. The screw tightening method applies to tightening the screws of the motor end cover and bearing cover; the operation should be carried out in sequence in the diagonal direction, with the purpose of preventing damage to the spigot fitting surface and avoiding eccentricity, and during tightening the rotor should be turned while observing its flexibility. Preventing foreign matter from being left requires that no foreign matter or parts be left in the motor during assembly; at the start of assembly, high-pressure air is generally used to blow out the motor interior once to remove foreign matter; all removed parts must be reinstalled, and if any are missing or extra, the cause must be found and the work redone.

Maintenance personnel are responsible for fault diagnosis and repair of marine electrical equipment, and need to be familiar with equipment principles, structures and maintenance materials; they are required to be familiar with the structure, working principles, functions of components (assemblies) and their locations of electrical equipment, and their methods can be summarized as asking, looking, touching, listening and smelling. In traditional fault diagnosis, it is necessary to understand the phenomenon and make a preliminary diagnosis through asking, looking, touching, listening and smelling; one must be very familiar with the ship's electrical equipment lines, working principles and the locations of all components. Interviewing the operator is the step preceding observation, and observation is the step preceding checking the monitoring and indicating device. When measuring resistance in a circuit, at least one end of the resistor under test must be completely disconnected from the circuit, and the power must be cut off; if there is capacitance in the circuit, it should be discharged before measuring resistance, and never measure with power on. Measuring current requires an ammeter, connected in series after breaking the circuit. In the fault of generator no-load voltage high, an open shunt resistor will cause the voltage regulator to lose its main regulating function. Poor contact is a fault state in which the contact resistance increases due to looseness, oxidation or foreign matter at an electrical connection; loose fastening screws and terminal lugs will cause poor contact and increase contact resistance; poor contact can cause local overheating, phase failure, phase-to-phase short circuit and other faults, and will cause random output actions, corresponding to checking the terminal wiring.

The root cause of armature winding overheating or burning is overcurrent. Long-term overload of the motor is one of the causes of overcurrent; short circuit of the armature winding or short circuit of the commutating-pole winding can cause overcurrent; when the load is unchanged, an applied voltage lower than the rated value will cause overcurrent; short circuit of the external load of the generator causes excessive current and hence overheating; frequent forward and reverse starting of the motor is one of the causes of overcurrent. Long-term overload in a DC machine is the cause of overcurrent in the armature winding and hence overheating, and is also one of the root causes of armature winding overheating; long-term overload will cause the temperature rise of the motor to be high and will cause the generator to overheat. Mutual rubbing between stator and rotor is one of the root causes of armature winding overheating because the resisting torque increases, and is also one of the causes of large armature vibration, usually induced by bearing wear or uneven clearance; mutual rubbing between stator and rotor will cause the generator to overheat. Stalling of the motor will cause the motor to overheat or even burn out; when the motor stalls, the stator current increases greatly; for example, for the domestic constant-power JZF-H type three-speed asynchronous motor, the stall currents of the 28-pole, 8-pole, and 4-pole versions are 2Ic, 4Ie, and 4Ie respectively, and the temperature rise is fast and high; a supply voltage that is too low or a starting resistance that is too large causes insufficient torque, resulting in stalling and failure to start of the DC motor. Poor contact between brush and commutator will cause low voltage or failure to build up voltage of the DC generator. In the fault of generator no-load voltage high, an open circuit in the shunt resistor will cause the voltage corrector to lose its main regulating action. The brush is an important conductive element used to lead current into or out of the stationary part and the sliding part of a machine; DC machines, AC wound-rotor motors, and synchronous generators all need to be equipped with brushes; commonly used brushes are divided into three types: graphite, electrographite, and metal graphite;

Loosening of the motor foot screws will cause large armature vibration and belongs to the mechanical fault causes induced by unbalance factors. Loosening of screws is a common cause in marine electrical and mechanical equipment in which vibration, improper installation, or ageing leads to a decrease in the tightening force of connecting parts, thereby causing poor contact, open circuit, overheating, or mechanical faults; in the fault of excessive temperature rise of the circuit breaker, loosening of the connecting screws of two conductive parts is one of the main causes and must be dealt with by tightening the screws; the vibration and swaying environment peculiar to ships can very easily cause loosening of the wiring clamping screws, the connections between cable lugs and the base, and the fixing screws inside the PLC, creating hidden fault risks.

The measurable property of resistance is the resistance value, and the measuring tool is the multimeter; the measurable property of voltage is the voltage value, and the measuring tools are the multimeter and the voltmeter; the measurable property of current is the current value, and the measuring tools are the multimeter, the ammeter, and the clamp-on ammeter. When measuring resistance in a measuring circuit, at least one end of the resistance under test must be completely disconnected from the circuit, and the power supply must be cut off; if there is a capacitor in the circuit, it should be discharged before measuring resistance, and never measure with the power on. To measure current, an ammeter must be used, and the circuit must be opened and the meter connected in series. Voltage is one of the instrument readings that need to be checked in marine electrical fault diagnosis; failure of the generator to build up voltage is an optional top event when constructing a fault tree; overvoltage is one of the causes of motor overheating in an AC electric drive system. Current is one of the electrical quantities measured by electrical instruments, used to understand equipment status; motor overcurrent is the direct cause of overheating of an AC electric drive system. Low supply voltage will cause loud noise of an AC electromagnet, that is, the fault cause of low supply voltage will make the AC electromagnet exhibit the phenomenon of loud noise; low supply voltage of an AC electromagnet will cause loud noise and must be adjusted to 0.85–1.10 times the rated voltage; the electrical cause of loud electromagnet noise, contact welding, low motor speed, and washing machine faults usually refers to a deviation below 85% of the rated value or exceeding 20%; when insufficient holding force of the contactor causes contact welding, it should be checked whether the supply voltage is lower than 85% of the rated voltage. A supply voltage that is too low will cause the electromagnetic force generated by the coil of the shunt trip unit of the circuit breaker to be insufficient, so that the circuit breaker cannot break; in a PLC control system, a supply voltage of the input signal source that is too low will cause all input terminals to fail to be connected.

Motor internal fault, apart from frequent operation and harsh working conditions, is also one of the causes of serious heating of the AC three-speed electric windlass; faults in the motor stator winding, bearing, etc. will also produce the phenomenon of excessively high temperature rise, and this fault will cause the motor to fail to start, automatic start failure, motor overheating, and even burning out; it is one of the direct causes of failure to start of the single-speed non-reversible AC electric drive system, belongs to one of the two major categories of common faults in the single-speed non-reversible direct-start control system on ships, is also one of the eight causes in the fault tree of failure to start of the three-phase asynchronous motor, and one of the eight possible causes of motor speed low after starting. When analyzing cage-type asynchronous motor faults, the fault tree (FTA) method is usually adopted, covering eight categories of causes such as starting failure and low speed.

The electric windlass may also exhibit the fault symptom of only medium and low speed and no high speed; this fault belongs to the common fault troubleshooting scope of the AC three-speed electric windlass, specifically manifested as the windlass motor having only medium and low speed positions available and completely lacking high-speed operating capability. Misoperation of the overcurrent relay GLJ will cause only medium and low speed and no high speed; poor contact of the contacts of the master controller, contactor and relay will cause only medium and low speed and no high speed; too short a delay setting of the 2SJ time relay will likewise cause only medium and low speed and no high speed. In addition, the electric windlass may also exhibit the fault symptom of brake failure.

Among the electrical faults of refrigeration and ventilation auxiliary equipment, compressor control failure is manifested as the compressor being unable to start and stop normally in the automatic operating state; during automatic operation the start and stop of the compressor are controlled by the suction-side low-pressure switch 63HL(L); if it can start normally after switching to manual, it is judged that the low-pressure switch 63HL(L) is damaged or its contacts are not closed. Too large a cold-storage temperature deviation is the temperature control deviation fault symptom occurring during operation of the refrigerating installation. Fan fault refers to the fan motor stall fault controlled by the defrost timer relay in the cold-storage refrigeration system; the fan operating state is controlled by the defrost timer relay, and the fan should run when not defrosting; if in the defrosting state, the fan stopping is a normal phenomenon rather than a fault. Failure of automatic defrosting is the fault symptom in refrigeration equipment in which the defrost timer or control switch fails, resulting in inability to defrost automatically; during troubleshooting, first place CS in the "DEFROST" position for manual defrosting to determine the fault scope; if manual defrosting succeeds, it indicates that the two sets of switches of the defrost timer cannot change over automatically.

If the AC three-speed electric windlass has only medium and low speed and no high speed at starting, troubleshooting can usually start from three clues. All three will cause no high speed at starting of the AC three-speed electric auxiliary machinery. Poor contact of contacts may alone cause no high speed, or may be one of the causes of misoperation of the overcurrent relay GLJ, and the two often occur together; when the delay setting of the 2SJ time relay is too short, the overcurrent protection is connected too early, likewise causing no high speed at starting, and this fault also often acts together with poor contact of contacts to make the protection operate too early. The zero-voltage relay may be set to 80% of rated voltage during debugging of auxiliary machinery.

The electric windlass may also suffer brake failure. If the brake is applied immediately after pickup at starting, it indicates that the economy resistor is open-circuited; if it cannot pick up and release the brake, first check whether 3SJ operates normally, then see whether 3SJ picks up when the master controller handle is in the zero position. If 3SJ picks up, it indicates that the brake power supply and 3SJ are normal, and at this time it should be checked whether the master controller contacts, the brake relay ZDC and the electromagnetic brake coil are open-circuited. If the brake cannot pick up and release the brake while 3SJ is normal, it should also be checked whether the master controller contacts have poor contact or are open-circuited. The mooring winch belongs to the short-time duty system equipment, and 25 starts within 30min are allowed;

The no-load test in auxiliary machinery debugging needs to observe the rotation direction, speed, and the starting current and no-load current of each position under step-by-step forward and reverse operation; in the final stage, the sound of the gearbox and bearing should be monitored at each position, and no-load operation for 2h should be carried out to check the temperature rise. In the no-load test, when the power supply is switched on and the master controller is in the zero position, only the zero-voltage relay and the time relay operate; no load applied corresponds to the step of switching on the power supply. After the windlass system has operated normally at no load for 2h, the insulation resistance needs to be measured at the final stop. Measuring insulation resistance is used to check the insulation performance of electrical equipment, often carried out after major overhaul of the equipment or after stopping following operation, and is also one of the mandatory inspection and test items for acceptance of a motor after new installation or major overhaul; in contactor maintenance, the insulation resistance between contacts, between the coil and the core, and between live parts and ground needs to be measured. The content of observation includes abnormal phenomena, part status, conductor status, and equipment operating parameters, specifically such as whether parts are jammed or damaged, and whether conductors are loose, broken, abraded or burnt. Checking the bearing fault and checking the lubrication condition are both carried out for the bearing;

The central unit adopts a modular structure; all circuit boards are installed in a standard frame, and corresponding modules can be selected according to system requirements. It includes components such as the alarm processing board, detection loop interface board, relay board, communication interface board, digital input board, digital output board, digital detector interface board and power supply unit. The alarm processing board is the core part of the central unit and is installed in the standard frame; the board contains one internal serial port and two external serial ports; the internal serial port is used to receive information from the detection loop interface board, the external serial ports are used to communicate with the operating unit, etc., and it completes the corresponding alarm and control functions through the relay board, realizes serial communication with external computers and printers through the communication interface board, and also controls the digital input board and digital output board to complete corresponding functions. The detection loop interface board is used to communicate with the detectors in the detection loop and transmit the detector status to the alarm processing board; each detection loop interface board can connect two detection loops, and a central unit can be inserted with up to 95 detection loop interface boards, whose addresses are set by the multi-row switch on the board. The relay board is controlled by the alarm processing board and drives equipment such as the general alarm system, alarm bell and fire door electromagnet; it has two DC 24V/2A power outputs and three DC 24V/2A active signal outputs, provides four relay signal outputs with a capacity of 2A, and has two signals output in open-collector form. The communication interface board is controlled by the alarm processing board; the board is equipped with two RS-232 and two RS-485 serial communication ports to realize serial communication with external computers and printers; it is a modular component of the central unit and is installed in a standard frame. The digital input board is controlled by the alarm processing board and can accept 24 channels of optoelectrically isolated digital signals; the digital output board is likewise controlled by the alarm processing board and can output 24 channels of optoelectrically isolated digital signals. The digital detector interface board is specially used to connect detection branches composed of non-intelligent digital detectors; the maximum connection capacity of a single digital detector interface board is four detection branches. The power supply unit includes two main components, the rectifier power supply and the storage battery, and has the functional characteristic of automatic switching between two power sources.

Regarding the detection loops, a central unit can connect up to 190 detection loops, each loop can be installed with 99 analog detectors or address units, and one loop can cover several decks of the ship; an ordinary two-core unshielded marine cable is sufficient to meet the requirements. The communication signals of the fire detection loop are specially processed, and an ordinary two-core unshielded marine cable can be used. The marine cable is composed of conductive copper core wires, an insulation layer and a protective layer, and is a component of marine electrical equipment; its conductive core wires are stranded from no fewer than seven round soft copper wires of 0.26～2.74mm to cope with vibration and impact on ships; the insulation layer electrically insulates the conductive core wires, and the protective layer is used to protect the cable from oil, water and chemical corrosion; when necessary, armoring is added to enhance mechanical strength and anti-interference capability; when selecting, attention must be paid to the selection principles of marine cables.

The operating unit is the device for human-machine dialogue between the operator and the system, and is composed of the fire alarm lamp, two groups of three-digit digital tubes, the liquid crystal display window and the keyboard. When a fire occurs in the system, the fire alarm lamp in the upper left corner flashes, and the two groups of three-digit digital tubes on the right respectively display the alarm loop number and detector number; the liquid crystal display window at the upper right displays system information, and the keyboard below is used to input control commands such as date, time and sensitivity. Some operating functions can only be realized after entering the corresponding password, so as to avoid misoperation by irrelevant personnel. When a system fault occurs, the fault lamp at the lower right flashes, and the corresponding light-emitting diode above it indicates the specific fault type. As a component of the fire monitoring system, the display shows regional fire alarm information in the central control room or accommodation area, and in the water mist fire-extinguishing system displays the release area on the bridge and gives an alarm indication;

In terms of electrical components, poor contact is a common cause of equipment failure. At electrical connections, looseness, oxidation or foreign matter causes the contact resistance to increase; loose fastening screws and wiring terminals will cause poor contact, increasing the contact resistance, which may in turn cause local overheating, phase failure, phase-to-phase short circuit and other faults. Common faults of the main engine remote control system include air leakage and jamming of pneumatic valves, poor contact of electrical components and incorrect parameter settings; in addition, there are fuse blowing or power supply faults, poor contact, corrosion of wiring terminals or connectors, electrical component faults, incorrect wiring after maintenance, etc.

As for fault diagnosis methods, fault tree analysis belongs to the analysis method for electronic equipment faults; in troubleshooting main engine starting faults it is also called the fault signal flow chart tracing method, which determines the fault location by tracing and checking the various signal paths and states related to the fault. Fault tree analysis uses a fault tree; a fault tree is a connected logic tree that contains no closed loops, using graphic symbols to represent the logical combination among various events that cause an undesired event of the system. Its structure is a logic tree, containing a top event, intermediate events and basic events; the logical relations are AND and OR, of which the most commonly used are the "AND" and "OR" relations. The fault tree develops downward from the tree root of the top event; the branches represent accident events and the nodes represent logical relations. Fault tree analysis originates from graph theory; it was first used by Bell Laboratories in the United States in the design of the Minuteman missile control system to predict random failure probability, and was later successfully applied in the WASH-1400 report, quantitatively giving the risk of nuclear power plants for the first time and proving their safety. The generator failing to build up voltage is also an optional top event when constructing a fault tree. Interviewing the operator is the step preceding observation, and observation is the step preceding checking the monitoring and indicating device. Overvoltage is one of the causes of motor overheating in AC electric drive systems. The secondary side must never be open-circuited; the external ammeter terminals must have a short-circuit connecting piece, and it may be disconnected only after the wiring has been checked.

Marine electrical equipment uses printed circuit boards; a printed circuit board consists of a base material and circuitry. The base material is an insulating material, most commonly phenolic resin board (yellow insulation) and epoxy glass (glass fiber); the circuitry part is made of conductive copper, laid on the base board, and the required pattern is obtained by etching. For printed circuit boards, attention must be paid to the soldering requirements for electronic components; the circuit board repair procedure applies to printed circuit boards; printed circuit boards will have circuit board faults, and circuit board faults fall within the scope of printed circuit boards.

The automatic fuel oil boiler contains a solenoid valve and a potentiometer. The sensor is placed in the X-axis and Y-axis directions, and its function is to send out an error signal;

For the daily maintenance of the main engine remote control system, the emphasis is on the cleanliness and sealing of the air circuit. The main engine remote control system can prevent faults by keeping the starting and control air supply pressure normal and clean, which is a basic task for preventing system faults. When disconnecting the air circuit piping, prevent external debris and dust from falling in. Daily maintenance requires keeping the air supply pressure normal and clean, periodically servicing the valves and checking the contact of the plugs.

During operation, the main engine remote control system will exhibit various fault symptoms: microcomputer remote control starting requires two attempts each time, the first fails and the second succeeds; after the main engine starts it cannot accelerate, and it automatically stops;

A fault in the logic circuit or logic relay circuit corresponds to the maintenance procedure of checking the logic circuit or logic relay circuit; this step addresses one of the possible causes of the main engine being unable to reverse by remote control. If a relay logic circuit is used, the relays of that part and their related contacts must be checked; if an electronic integrated circuit is used, it can be checked by the waveform test method or the substitution method; in a microcomputer control system, the fault point can be found through the self-test program or the simulation board function; when all starting conditions are met and the detecting elements are normal, the discrimination circuit inside the control box should be checked. A fault in the machinery and control detection circuits or devices that prevent reversing to a certain direction corresponds to the maintenance procedure of detecting the machinery and control detection circuits or devices that prevent reversing to a certain direction; this fault is one of the possible causes of the main engine being unable to reverse by remote control, and the fault manifests as failure to correctly respond to the reversing state or command; during maintenance, emphasis should be placed on detecting the relevant machinery and control detection components and circuits; this link belongs to the fault of the execution and feedback link outside the logic discrimination circuit of the remote control system.

In the main engine remote control system, the pressure reducing valve realizes the setting of working parameters such as the starting fuel quantity through the set value; in normal use the output air pressure is stable at the set value, and when the working condition is unchanged no air escapes from the vent hole;

For the daily maintenance of the main engine remote control system, the emphasis is on keeping the starting and control air supply pressure normal and clean, and periodically inspecting the interior of the control box and the input and output components, confirming before sailing the items that must be checked in conjunction with main engine preparation as well as the content of the periodic effectiveness test of all system functions. Specifically, the main engine remote control system can prevent faults caused by unclean air supply or abnormal pressure, by the method of keeping the starting and control air supply pressure normal and clean; the main engine remote control system can prevent faults inside the control box caused by dust accumulation, dampness or looseness, by the method of inspection inside the control box; the main engine remote control system can prevent faults of input and output components caused by poor contact or damage, by the method of inspecting the input and output components; the main engine remote control system can prevent hidden dangers being discovered only after sailing, by the method of confirming item by item before sailing the items that must be checked in conjunction with main engine preparation; the main engine remote control system can prevent function failure, by the method of periodically carrying out the effectiveness test of all system functions.

The types of main engine remote control systems cover the pneumatic main engine remote control system, the electric main engine remote control system, the electro-pneumatic main engine remote control system, the electro-hydraulic main engine remote control system, the microcomputer-controlled main engine remote control system and the field bus type main engine remote control system; the NABCO M-800 main engine remote control system is one of them; in addition there are the digital electronic governor system and the main engine remote control system simulation test device. It may also happen that after the main engine starts it cannot accelerate, and it automatically stops;

The control modes of the NABCO M-800 main engine remote control system include microcomputer remote control, pneumatic remote control and local control; normal use is microcomputer remote control, pneumatic remote control serves as backup, and local control is used only as emergency use. This system has a two-attempt starting mechanism; after the first failure it automatically performs the second Heavy Start; the starting fuel quantity air pressure set by the simulation board for the first start is 1.1bar, and the Heavy Start starting fuel quantity set air pressure set on the panel for the second start is
1.8bar. When the fault occurs that microcomputer remote control starting requires two attempts each time, the first failing and the second succeeding, while local control and pneumatic remote control modes are both normal, parameters and safety protection devices show no abnormality, and simulation board tests show no hardware fault in the microcomputer and control valve themselves, the root cause is that the first-start starting fuel quantity air pressure set value of 1.1bar is lower than the second Heavy Start set value of 1.8bar, that is, the starting fuel quantity is too small. After 10 years of operation of this engine, due to wear of the cylinders and the fuel throttle transmission mechanism, continuing to use the new-engine starting fuel quantity parameters will result in it being too small; at this time the remedy corresponding to the starting fuel quantity being too small is to set the starting fuel quantity air pressure of the first start to 1.5bar, that is, adjust the starting fuel quantity air pressure of the first start from 1.1bar to 1.5bar; the second start automatically switches to the Heavy Start mode, and starting succeeds using the 1.8bar speed setting air pressure signal, and the fault is thus eliminated. The original setting of 1.1bar is the new-engine standard, and after 10 years of operation it needs to be appropriately modified according to the machine condition.

Although the above causes have similar manifestations, the corresponding treatment measures are different from one another.

For the fault of one phase open circuit in the phase-shifting reactor, rectifier or phase compound excitation transformer, the open-circuit point of the wiring among the three should be checked during troubleshooting and repaired. This fault involves one phase open circuit in one of the phase-shifting reactor, rectifier and phase compound excitation transformer; the inspection focuses on the open-circuit point of the wiring among the above three and repairing it; if it is confirmed to be an internal open circuit of the rectifier, the rectifier and its wiring need to be checked and made conductive. An open circuit in the rectifier is one of the specific fault causes listed in the troubleshooting of phase compound excitation generator faults, and its corresponding troubleshooting method is to check the rectifier and its wiring and make them conductive; the core of the operation is to check the state of the rectifier itself and its connecting lines, and the ultimate purpose is to reconnect the disconnected rectifier circuit. This cause is parallel to phase-shifting reactor open circuit, inconsistent polarity of the phase compound excitation transformer windings, and others.

Inconsistent polarity of the current winding and voltage winding of the phase compound excitation transformer is manifested as inconsistent polarity of the current winding and voltage winding of the phase compound excitation transformer, and the troubleshooting method is to swap the current winding or voltage winding so that the polarities of the two are consistent. This fault is item 3 in the troubleshooting list of faults of generator-related equipment (such as the phase-shifting reactor and rectifier). For the fault of poor speed regulation performance of the prime mover, the measure of overhauling the governor should be taken; poor speed regulation performance of the prime mover is one of the causes listed in generator fault troubleshooting, and overhauling the governor is measure 4 in the troubleshooting methods for generator-related faults. Displacement of the stator core is one of the causes listed in the phase compound excitation generator fault troubleshooting table; for the fault of stator core displacement, the core needs to be adjusted back to its original position and fixed securely, and this step includes the two actions of adjusting the core back to its original position and fixing it securely, and is a specific maintenance measure in the generator fault troubleshooting methods.

In the diagnostic process, voltage and current are two basic measurable quantities. The core is a component of marine electrical equipment, and the contactor contains the core; the phase-shifting reactor is used to regulate voltage, and if its air gap is too large, it will cause the generator terminal voltage to be higher than the rated value;

When the motor temperature is too high or it smokes, the corresponding treatment measure is to remove objects hindering ventilation and clean the dust and dirt at the coil ends.

In the troubleshooting of the ship's power station, the conventional fault diagnosis method takes observation as the first step, and before observation the operator is usually interviewed first to understand the operating conditions and abnormal manifestations before and after the fault occurs, that is, interviewing the operator is the preceding step of observation. The content of observation covers abnormal phenomena, part conditions, conductor conditions and equipment operating parameters, specifically including whether parts are stuck or damaged, and whether conductors are loose, broken, abraded or burnt. After observation, the step of checking the monitoring and indicating device is entered, and observation is the preceding step of checking the monitoring and indicating device. During observation, instruments are needed to measure electrical quantities; voltage and current are both objects measured by electrical instruments; the multimeter can be used to measure voltage values and current values, and can also be used to test diodes and thyristors;

Generator bearing overheating is one of the common faults of the uncontrollable phase compound excitation voltage regulation system, manifested as an abnormal rise in bearing temperature, and its causes can be summarized in three aspects: bearing fault, lubrication fault and installation fault. As far as lubrication is concerned, too much, too little or deteriorated lubricating oil (grease) will all cause generator bearing overheating, and when adding oil the oil quantity must not exceed 2/3 of the bearing chamber space. For this cause, the treatment corresponding to generator bearing overheating is to check and add or change oil, and the lubricating oil quantity must not exceed 2/3 of the bearing chamber space. As far as installation is concerned, improper assembly of the motor end cover or bearing cover and poor assembly of the generator set will both cause bearing overheating; the former requires reinstallation, and the latter requires realignment and reinstallation. A bent generator shaft will likewise cause bearing overheating, and the treatment method is to straighten the shaft. The generator bearing is used to support the generator shaft; a marine motor usually contains components such as the bearing, winding and shaft, among which the winding is wound from enameled wire and used to generate a magnetic field or induce an electromotive force; the insulation of the marine motor winding must undergo the "three-proof" treatment of moisture proofing, mildew proofing and salt-oil-mist proofing and be oil resistant; the shaft is used to transmit torque and rotational motion;

Aiming at the problem that insulating materials are prone to mold growth due to ambient temperature changes, heaters are often added on ships to raise the ambient temperature. The maintenance requirement for such electrical equipment with a heating link is regular maintenance and energization for operation, the purpose being to prevent condensation and drive out moisture, thereby achieving a mold-proof effect. In the use of marine electrical equipment, attention must be paid to the maintenance item of maintenance of electrical equipment with a heating link, that is, the electrical equipment with added heaters on ships is maintained periodically and energized for operation, and the energization of the heaters makes the internal temperature of the equipment higher than the surrounding environment, avoiding water vapor condensing into dew on the insulating surface, and at the same time driving out moisture that has already penetrated, so that mold loses the conditions for breeding. In the use of marine electrical equipment, attention must also be paid to the precautions for use of electric soldering irons, involving the operating specifications during welding operations.

In the actual selection and repair judgment of marine insulating materials, insulation performance often needs to be indirectly confirmed through electrical quantities, among which voltage is the most basic measured quantity. The tool used to measure voltage values is the multimeter, and a voltmeter can also be used to complete the same reading task. For marine electrical equipment, voltage is not only the direct basis for judging whether a circuit is energized and whether insulation has been broken down, but is also one of the instrument readings that need to be checked in fault diagnosis;

Polyester film is one of the more widely used marine solid insulating materials. When selecting, thickness, insulation strength and heat resistance class should be checked in combination, avoiding looking at only one of them and ignoring the rest.

When identifying the positive and negative poles, the long pin is the positive pole; it has three pins, among which the resistance between two pins is fixed, and the resistance between the third pin and either pin changes with the rotation of the shaft. In the differential amplifier circuit, the potentiometer Rp is often used as the zero-adjustment element;

The resistor category also includes photoresistors and thermistors.

Photosensitive devices include photosensitive batteries, photoresistors, photodiodes, and phototransistors, etc., which can convert optical signals into electrical signals. The measurable properties include resistance change, electromotive force, and current. Devices such as photoresistors use the photoconductive characteristics of semiconductors; the resistance changes with the intensity of incident light. When the light intensity increases, the resistance decreases significantly, and when the light intensity decreases, the resistance increases significantly. Photosensitive batteries generate electromotive force or current after being exposed to light, which can be detected with the DC mV/mA range of a multimeter.

A large quantity of this type of electrical apparatus is used on ships, and the overload protection task of small motors is generally performed by it. If the terminal is in poor contact, the heat generated will cause an error in the operating value, so the terminal must be kept in good contact. When the bimetallic strip undergoes permanent deformation due to a huge short-circuit current, only a thermal relay of the same model can be replaced, and calibration cannot be performed.

The thermal relay not operating is one of the common fault symptoms. A setting value that is too large is one of the main causes of this symptom, and during handling it must be reset according to the rated current of the motor. Jamming of the operating mechanism can likewise cause refusal to operate, and during maintenance the cover plate must be opened to eliminate the fault, and after repair a manual test must also be performed to confirm flexible operation. Dust accumulation or rust, and wear and deformation of the transmission mechanism can all cause the thermal relay not to operate; for dust accumulation or rust and wear and deformation of the transmission mechanism, the maintenance method is to remove dust and rust or replace the thermal relay. Damage to adjustable parts or failure to align with the scale will cause the setting to fail and the relay not to operate, and usually the thermal relay also needs to be replaced. If the setting value is incorrectly raised, for example adjusted to 2, the thermal relay will not operate, thereby causing fuse blowing or the motor to smoke.

After the thermal relay is connected, the main circuit is not energized; one of its fault causes is burning out of the heating element, and the second cause is that the external wiring screws are not tightened. If it is caused by burning out of the heating element, the maintenance method is to replace the thermal relay;

The object targeted by contactor maintenance is the contactor.

The pressure relay takes pressure as its input signal and belongs to the non-electrical quantity relays; its sensing element is an air bag, and it is used for automatic control and protection of ship boilers, air compressors and pump sets; its actuating part is the contact, and in auxiliary boilers it is used as a control apparatus to automatically control steam pressure. The temperature relay is likewise a non-electrical quantity relay, and can serve as both a control apparatus and a protection apparatus; it is commonly used in ship cooling water temperature alarms and cargo hold temperature regulation, and is used in relatively large quantities on ships; when used for cooling water temperature alarm it performs a protection function, and when used in auxiliary boilers and similar occasions it can serve as a control apparatus.

The starting box commissioning steps apply to the air compressor control circuit. Before formally energizing, check whether the starting box meets the requirements; the model and capacity of the starting box should be consistent with the matching motor; the inside and outside of the starting box should be intact and free of oil dirt and contaminants, and the internal wiring should conform to the drawings;

Before using the keyboard to modify the system operating parameters or modifying the system operating parameters through the trimming potentiometer on the printed circuit board, it is best to record the original data or original position. When performing electrical measurements, the test plan should be designed in advance. When inserting or removing the printed circuit board, turn off the power and remember the model of the printed circuit board and its original position in the slot.

Preventive maintenance and inspection items. The preventive work of the main engine remote control system includes: keeping the starting and control air supply pressure normal and clean; carrying out internal inspection of the control box; carrying out inspection of input and output components; the items to be checked in conjunction with main engine preparation before sailing; and periodically carrying out effectiveness tests of all system functions.

Common fault symptoms. Various fault symptoms will appear during operation of the main engine remote control system, including: microcomputer remote control starting requires two attempts each time, the first fails and the second succeeds; the main engine cannot accelerate after starting and automatically stops; serious air leakage from the vent holes of the pressure reducing valve and pressure regulating valve; air leakage in the pneumatic logic circuit;

The automatic fuel oil boiler includes a solenoid valve and a potentiometer. The sensor is placed in the X-axis and Y-axis directions, and its function is to send out an error signal;

The daily maintenance of the main engine remote control system focuses on the cleanliness and sealing of the air circuit. The main engine remote control system can prevent faults by keeping the starting and control air supply pressure normal and clean, which is a basic task for preventing system faults. When disconnecting air circuit piping, prevent external debris and dust from falling in. Daily maintenance requires keeping the air supply pressure normal and clean, periodically servicing valve components and checking plug-in contact.

During operation, the main engine remote control system will exhibit various fault symptoms: microcomputer remote control starting requires two attempts each time, with the first failing and the second succeeding; after the main engine starts, it cannot accelerate and automatically stops;

main engine cannot be started by remote control; cannot restart; cannot reverse by remote control. These symptoms involve the air circuit, electrical circuit, mechanical aspects and wiring, and need to be reproduced and judged one by one in the simulation test. Moving parts jammed will prevent valves or actuators from reaching position. Electrical component fault will make the corresponding function fail;

In the pneumatic and mechanical links, the main engine remote control system includes specific components such as the reversing solenoid valve and orifice 3. Whether the reversing solenoid valve operates normally directly affects the execution of the reversing command; the flow state of orifice 3 relates to the changes of pressure and flow in the air circuit.

In electrical measurement and fault diagnosis, voltage is an instrument reading that needs to be checked. The voltage value can be measured with a multimeter or a voltmeter; the multimeter is used in practice to measure voltage. When measuring, the range should be selected according to the expected voltage at the measured point, and attention should be paid to the polarity of the test probes and the earthing relationship.

During troubleshooting, resistance and voltage are two basic parameters that need to be measured. The measurable property of a resistor is resistance, and the measuring tool is a multimeter; if its air gap is too small, it will cause the generator terminal voltage to be lower than the rated value;

As for the bearing itself, bearing damage will cause bearing overheating, and severe bearing wear will directly cause generator bearing overheating; as for lubrication, insufficient lubricating oil, impurities, and too much, too little or deteriorated lubricating oil (grease) will all cause generator bearing overheating; as for installation, improper assembly of the motor end cover or bearing cover, poor assembly of the generator set, and bending of the generator shaft will likewise cause generator bearing overheating. A marine motor includes a bearing and a shaft; both the brushed synchronous generator and the brushless synchronous generator also include a generator bearing and a generator shaft. The generator bearing undertakes the task of supporting the generator shaft, and the maintenance and upkeep of the generator bearing are carried out precisely for this component, the generator bearing.

For bearing overheating, the troubleshooting work corresponds to three directions: checking bearing fault, checking the lubrication condition, and checking the installation condition. Checking the lubrication condition also requires the use of the bearing, checking the amount and quality of the lubricating grease from the bearing; checking the installation condition focuses on the assembly state of the end cover, bearing cover and the unit as a whole.

The illuminance of the landing floor should be at least 50lx, so that users can see the area clearly when the car lighting fails. Passenger elevators are not allowed to be frequently used as freight elevators, and it is strictly prohibited to transport flammable and explosive dangerous goods. If the elevator is out of use for more than one week, it must be carefully inspected and trial-run before use.

The elevator includes components such as the traction sheave, brake wheel, brake shoe, speed governor, safety gear, guide rail, buffer, traction steel wire rope, traveling cable and counterweight device, and its maintenance work covers traction machine maintenance, traction machine lubrication, brake system maintenance, speed governor inspection, car door and automatic door mechanism inspection and adjustment, safety gear inspection, guide rail and guide shoe inspection, buffer inspection, and landing door mechanical and electrical interlock device inspection, etc. During elevator repair, attention should be paid to the regulations to be observed during elevator repair and maintenance, the regulations on the use of the insulation resistance tester during elevator repair and maintenance, the safety measures before brake system adjustment, the safety measures before measuring the thickness of the brake lining, and the safety measures during inspection and repair.

The regulations to be observed during elevator repair and maintenance require: during repair, no passengers or cargo may be carried, and inspection/out-of-service indication signs must be hung at each landing door;

For the fault of relatively large temperature variation, external high temperature (such as high seawater temperature) will affect the cooling effect and cause the room temperature to rise; this fault is likely to occur when a ship passes through high-temperature sea areas such as the Red Sea and the Persian Gulf in summer. Insufficient refrigerant caused by refrigerant leakage or low efficiency caused by poor compressor maintenance are common causes. Placing the unloading selector switch in the unloading position for manual unloading operation will cause insufficient cooling capacity, and misoperation of automatic unloading switches 63CU1/63CU2 will cause unexpected unloading operation.

For the failure to start fault, possible causes include the power supply not being connected, incorrect wiring of control equipment, undervoltage, short circuit to earth between stator winding phases, incorrect wiring, stator winding short circuit, excessive load or the brake not being released, and faults in the reducer. Corresponding treatment measures include: check the switch, fuse and each pair of contacts to find the fault point; check against the wiring diagram and correct it; check the power supply voltage and, if it is indeed too low, raise it appropriately;

Poor contact is a fault state in which the contact resistance at an electrical connection increases due to looseness, oxidation or foreign matter. Loose fastening screws and terminal ends will cause poor contact and increase contact resistance. Poor contact can cause local overheating, phase failure, phase-to-phase short circuit and other faults.

The elevator is a commonly used living facility on ships, consisting of a traction machine, car, landing door and electrical control system, and requires regular maintenance to ensure safe operation;

The temperature controller is an electrical control component in the refrigeration system of refrigerators and refrigerated containers, which monitors temperature through a temperature-sensing element and controls the start and stop of the compressor. When terminals 10 and 11 of electronic temperature controller 23A1 are conducting, the liquid supply solenoid valve is energized and opens, and the compressor starts refrigerating; when the temperature inside the container reaches the set lower limit, terminals 10 and 11 of electronic temperature controller 23A1 are disconnected, and the compressor stops running; when defrost timer 2DX operates, its normally closed contact cuts off the circuit between the electronic temperature controller and the liquid supply solenoid valve to perform defrosting. In a refrigerator, if the motor does not start and there is no sound after power-on, it may be because the temperature controller is disconnected or in the "stop point" position; leakage loss of the temperature-sensing agent in the temperature controller will cause an open circuit, resulting in the refrigerator motor failing to start, and the temperature-sensing agent needs to be reset or the thermostat replaced.

Motor overload is a fault cause that needs to be prevented in the control circuit of refrigeration equipment.

Circuit breaker maintenance applies to the circuit breaker itself.

The circuit breaker may have the fault of the contacts failing to close when the circuit breaker is operated manually, that is, when the circuit breaker is operated manually, its contacts cannot close into position. In addition to these two cases, the circuit breaker may also have the faults of one phase contact failing to close, the shunt trip unit failing to make the circuit breaker open, the undervoltage trip unit failing to make the circuit breaker open, the circuit breaker tripping immediately when starting the motor, the circuit breaker automatically opening about 1h after closing, noise from the undervoltage trip unit, excessive temperature rise of the circuit breaker, failure of the auxiliary switch, and maloperation of the semiconductor overcurrent trip unit causing the circuit breaker to open.

As far as the circuit breaker is concerned, the contacts failing to close when the circuit breaker is operated manually is a common fault, and the contacts failing to close when the circuit breaker is operated electrically is another common fault; when troubleshooting, the two should first be distinguished by whether the operation mode is manual or electric, and then the corresponding transmission and tripping links should be checked separately. In addition, the circuit breaker may also have the case of one phase contact failing to close; at this time, focus on checking that phase contact and its linkage components;
